1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,350
Horsburgh Lighthouse: Building
the Light on Pedra Branca

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:08,564
On the 24th of May 1850,
Queen Victoria's birthday,

3
00:00:08,644 --> 00:00:11,931
a steam frigate towed two ships out to a

4
00:00:12,011 --> 00:00:15,800
bare granite rock 37
miles east of Singapore.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:20,151
On board were the Governor of the
Straits Settlements, an admiral,

6
00:00:20,231 --> 00:00:24,717
the foreign consuls and the Freemasons
of Lodge Zetland in the East,

7
00:00:24,797 --> 00:00:28,825
who had come to lay a foundation
stone with Masonic honours.

8
00:00:28,825 --> 00:00:33,518
The rock was Pedra Branca, and the
stone was the first of a lighthouse

9
00:00:33,598 --> 00:00:37,825
that would carry the name of the
hydrographer James Horsburgh.

10
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:41,795
Its architect, the government
surveyor John Turnbull Thomson,

11
00:00:41,875 --> 00:00:46,508
wrote a long account of how it was
built, and more than 150 years later

12
00:00:46,588 --> 00:00:51,089
the building and running of that
lighthouse were part of the evidence

13
00:00:51,169 --> 00:00:55,550
when the International Court of
Justice decided who owns the rock.

14
00:00:55,550 --> 00:00:58,854
Pedra Branca means "white
rock" in Portuguese,

15
00:00:58,934 --> 00:01:03,200
and the Malays called it Batu
Putih, which means the same.

16
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:06,278
Thomson wrote that before
the work began the rock

17
00:01:06,358 --> 00:01:09,437
looked perfectly white,
because it was covered in

18
00:01:09,517 --> 00:01:12,675
the droppings of the sea
birds that rested there.

19
00:01:12,675 --> 00:01:16,209
It sits at the eastern entrance
to the Singapore Strait,

20
00:01:16,289 --> 00:01:19,630
nine miles from the nearest
point of the Johor coast,

21
00:01:19,710 --> 00:01:22,470
and for centuries it
was the mark that ships

22
00:01:22,550 --> 00:01:26,100
steered for when they came
in from the South China Sea.

23
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:29,505
Portuguese sailing
directions from the 1500s

24
00:01:29,585 --> 00:01:33,150
already named it as the
mark for the channel.

25
00:01:33,150 --> 00:01:36,075
It was also a dangerous place.

26
00:01:36,075 --> 00:01:40,302
Thomson went through every
Singapore newspaper published

27
00:01:40,382 --> 00:01:45,150
since 1824 and listed the ships
lost or damaged near the rock.

28
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:49,686
Between 1824 and 1839 there
were five total wrecks,

29
00:01:49,766 --> 00:01:53,669
and between 1841 and
1851 there were eleven,

30
00:01:53,749 --> 00:01:58,275
about one a year, as trade
through Singapore grew.

31
00:01:58,275 --> 00:02:00,310
One of them, the Dourado,

32
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:04,450
went down with 500,000
Spanish dollars on board.

33
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:09,150
James Horsburgh had spent his
career making these waters safer.

34
00:02:09,150 --> 00:02:12,973
He was a Scottish hydrographer
to the East India Company,

35
00:02:13,053 --> 00:02:16,123
and his charts and sailing
directions were the

36
00:02:16,203 --> 00:02:19,900
ones that ships' captains
carried in the Eastern seas.

37
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:22,970
After he died in 1836, money was raised by

38
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:26,870
subscription for a memorial
to him, and the memorial

39
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:30,625
became a lighthouse, the
"Horsburgh Testimonial".

40
00:02:30,625 --> 00:02:35,116
Of the final cost, $9,884 came
from private subscriptions,

41
00:02:35,196 --> 00:02:41,814
much of it gathered through the trading firm
Jardine, Matheson and Company of Canton,

42
00:02:41,894 --> 00:02:46,150
and the East India Company's
government paid the rest.

43
00:02:46,150 --> 00:02:51,638
Thomson had first drawn plans in
1844 for a lighthouse on Peak Rock,

44
00:02:51,718 --> 00:02:55,975
one of the Romania islands
close to the Johor shore.

45
00:02:55,975 --> 00:03:01,189
In June 1847 the government told him
it had chosen Pedra Branca instead,

46
00:03:01,269 --> 00:03:03,469
on the advice of the Admiralty,

47
00:03:03,549 --> 00:03:08,475
because it stood further out and
ships could steer straight for it.

48
00:03:08,475 --> 00:03:11,773
To find out how hard the sea hit the rock,

49
00:03:11,853 --> 00:03:15,875
he had brick pillars built
on it in November 1847.

50
00:03:15,875 --> 00:03:20,136
When he went back in March 1848,
the ones on the north side,

51
00:03:20,216 --> 00:03:22,669
some of them 13 feet above the sea,

52
00:03:22,749 --> 00:03:27,010
had been swept away, so he
designed a tower built of granite

53
00:03:27,090 --> 00:03:30,925
from its foundation to the
top, not just at its base.

54
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:34,124
The East India Company's directors

55
00:03:34,204 --> 00:03:37,675
approved his plans in December 1849.

56
00:03:37,675 --> 00:03:41,868
The granite was not cut on the
rock but at quarries on Pulau Ubin,

57
00:03:41,948 --> 00:03:44,911
by Chinese stonecutters
who dressed each block,

58
00:03:44,991 --> 00:03:48,018
laid out every course of
the tower in the quarry

59
00:03:48,098 --> 00:03:51,400
to check the fit, and then
shipped the courses out.

60
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:53,846
A good stonecutter, Thomson noted,

61
00:03:53,926 --> 00:03:56,600
earned nine Spanish dollars a month.

62
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:00,290
The first party went out
at the end of March 1850:

63
00:04:00,370 --> 00:04:05,950
eleven Indian convicts to work as
quarrymen and three Chinese blacksmiths.

64
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:09,404
The day after they landed,
the north-east wind rose so

65
00:04:09,484 --> 00:04:12,546
quickly that the gunboat
had to run for shelter,

66
00:04:12,626 --> 00:04:16,145
and the men were left on the
rock with two days' water,

67
00:04:16,225 --> 00:04:19,825
waving handkerchiefs on sticks
when the ship came back.

68
00:04:19,825 --> 00:04:21,879
In the end they were hauled off

69
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:24,575
through the surf on a line and a buoy.

70
00:04:24,575 --> 00:04:27,661
When the work began in
earnest in April 1850,

71
00:04:27,741 --> 00:04:32,586
Thomson listed everyone on the rock:
Chinese stonecutters, carpenters,

72
00:04:32,666 --> 00:04:36,666
blacksmiths, coolies and a
cook, Indian convict quarrymen,

73
00:04:36,746 --> 00:04:40,184
Malay sailors from the
gunboat, a Hindu cook and a

74
00:04:40,264 --> 00:04:44,194
Muslim table servant, 46 people
in all, living in huts of

75
00:04:44,274 --> 00:04:48,425
plank and attap on a rock about
450 feet long at low water.

76
00:04:48,425 --> 00:04:52,475
In June the contractor,
Choah Ah Lam, disappeared.

77
00:04:52,475 --> 00:04:56,974
Thomson found that he had boarded
an American ship bound for China,

78
00:04:57,054 --> 00:05:00,950
and from then on he paid the
workmen daily wages himself.

79
00:05:00,950 --> 00:05:04,714
The number of men at
the Pulau Ubin quarries

80
00:05:04,794 --> 00:05:08,375
rose from about 20 to between 80 and 100.

81
00:05:08,375 --> 00:05:12,094
The waters around the rock
had a reputation for piracy,

82
00:05:12,174 --> 00:05:17,068
and Thomson designed the tower itself
as a strongpoint, with thick doors

83
00:05:17,148 --> 00:05:21,500
and room inside for six months
of stores, water and provisions.

84
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:24,758
Two armed gunboats served
the works, and no pirate

85
00:05:24,838 --> 00:05:27,775
came near the rock while
the men were there.

86
00:05:27,775 --> 00:05:32,180
Boats belonging to the stonecutters
were less lucky: three of the

87
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:36,666
Pulau Ubin men were killed in
attacks, and Thomson heard of eight

88
00:05:36,746 --> 00:05:41,300
more killed on the 15 miles between
Singapore town and Pulau Ubin.

89
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:47,254
By the time the north-east monsoon
closed the first season in October 1850,

90
00:05:47,334 --> 00:05:52,000
the shaft of the tower stood
59 feet above its foundation.

91
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,900
Work resumed in April 1851.

92
00:05:55,900 --> 00:06:00,015
The lantern and the lighting
apparatus, designed in Scotland

93
00:06:00,095 --> 00:06:04,280
by Alan Stevenson, engineer to
the Northern Lighthouse Board,

94
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:07,985
were landed on the 27th of
August, and on the 27th of

95
00:06:08,065 --> 00:06:12,400
September the Governor came out
to inspect the finished tower.

96
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:16,929
That evening the light was lit for
the first time, for a few hours,

97
00:06:17,009 --> 00:06:20,299
and Thomson watched it
from the departing steamer

98
00:06:20,379 --> 00:06:24,575
until it sank below the horizon
about 15 nautical miles away.

99
00:06:24,575 --> 00:06:30,392
The whole work had cost 23,665
Spanish dollars and 87 cents,

100
00:06:30,472 --> 00:06:34,600
not counting the gunboats and the steamer.

101
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:38,070
On the 6th of October 1851 the Singapore

102
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:41,700
Free Press printed a Notice to Mariners.

103
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:47,756
It said the light would first be shown
on the night of the 15th of October 1851,

104
00:06:47,836 --> 00:06:51,825
"and every night thereafter
from sunset to sunrise".

105
00:06:51,825 --> 00:06:55,945
It would be a revolving light
that grew to its brightest

106
00:06:56,025 --> 00:07:00,295
once a minute and then faded,
set 95 feet above high water

107
00:07:00,375 --> 00:07:04,800
and visible from the deck of
a ship 15 nautical miles away.

108
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:07,870
The notice listed every
rock and shoal within

109
00:07:07,950 --> 00:07:11,100
reach of the light, with
its compass bearing.

110
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:15,144
The same page reported that
the English barque Metropolis,

111
00:07:15,224 --> 00:07:19,775
a tea ship, had been wrecked on
nearby Bintan some weeks before.

112
00:07:19,775 --> 00:07:22,760
The last of the workmen left the

113
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:26,000
rock on the 22nd of October 1851.

114
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,625
Life on the rock stayed hard.

115
00:07:28,625 --> 00:07:33,852
In 1950, when the lighthouse turned
100, Singapore's Master Attendant,

116
00:07:33,932 --> 00:07:38,021
Commander L. P. Lane, described
it in the Straits Times

117
00:07:38,101 --> 00:07:42,650
as the oldest and most isolated
of the colony's lighthouses.

118
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:47,182
Its crew of seven, a keeper, a
serang (boatswain), a leading hand,

119
00:07:47,262 --> 00:07:50,825
three hands and a cook,
were relieved once a month.

120
00:07:50,825 --> 00:07:54,176
During the north-east
monsoon, six months of

121
00:07:54,256 --> 00:07:58,308
rations were landed at once,
and in 1941 it had taken

122
00:07:58,388 --> 00:08:01,975
five days to get a relief
party onto the rock.

123
00:08:01,975 --> 00:08:05,550
Keepers at the other lights
could bring their families,

124
00:08:05,630 --> 00:08:07,025
but not at Horsburgh.

125
00:08:07,025 --> 00:08:11,635
By then the light burned kerosene
and turned on clockwork machinery

126
00:08:11,715 --> 00:08:15,775
that Chance Brothers had
installed about 50 years earlier.

127
00:08:15,775 --> 00:08:20,306
Lane gave it a strength
of 154,500 candle-power,

128
00:08:20,386 --> 00:08:23,188
still visible at 15 miles and,

129
00:08:23,268 --> 00:08:27,975
on a clear night, glowing
over the horizon at 25.

130
00:08:27,975 --> 00:08:31,396
Many of the station's
older records, he noted,

131
00:08:31,476 --> 00:08:34,900
had been lost during
the Japanese Occupation.

132
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:40,973
For more than a century after 1851 the
British ran the lighthouse from Singapore,

133
00:08:41,053 --> 00:08:44,775
and Singapore kept running
it after independence.

134
00:08:44,775 --> 00:08:48,527
The question of who owns
the rock became a public

135
00:08:48,608 --> 00:08:51,421
dispute on the 21st of December 1979,

136
00:08:51,501 --> 00:08:55,723
when Malaysia published a
map that showed Pedra Branca,

137
00:08:55,803 --> 00:09:01,200
which Malaysia calls Pulau Batu
Puteh, inside its territorial waters.

138
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:04,149
Singapore protested by diplomatic

139
00:09:04,229 --> 00:09:07,350
note on the 14th of February 1980.

140
00:09:07,350 --> 00:09:12,263
Talks in 1993 and 1994 did not
settle the matter, and on the 6th of

141
00:09:12,343 --> 00:09:17,108
February 2003 the two countries
signed a Special Agreement to ask

142
00:09:17,188 --> 00:09:21,654
the International Court of
Justice in The Hague to decide it,

143
00:09:21,734 --> 00:09:27,100
together with two smaller features
nearby, Middle Rocks and South Ledge.

144
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:31,600
The two sides read the history
of the lighthouse differently.

145
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:35,628
Malaysia argued that the island
had always been part of Johor and

146
00:09:35,708 --> 00:09:39,483
that Britain had built and kept
the lighthouse there with the

147
00:09:39,563 --> 00:09:44,050
permission of the territorial sovereign,
which did not make it British.

148
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:47,846
Singapore argued that the
building of the lighthouse

149
00:09:47,926 --> 00:09:52,319
between 1847 and 1851 had been
a lawful taking of possession

150
00:09:52,399 --> 00:09:56,568
by the British Crown, and that
Britain and then Singapore

151
00:09:56,648 --> 00:10:00,375
had acted as sovereign
over the island ever since.

152
00:10:00,375 --> 00:10:05,450
The Court gave its judgment
on the 23rd of May 2008.

153
00:10:05,450 --> 00:10:08,248
It found that the Sultanate of Johor

154
00:10:08,328 --> 00:10:11,525
had held original title to Pedra Branca.

155
00:10:11,525 --> 00:10:14,359
It then looked at what both sides had done

156
00:10:14,439 --> 00:10:17,075
over the following century and a half.

157
00:10:17,075 --> 00:10:21,198
In 1953 Singapore's Colonial
Secretary had written to the

158
00:10:21,278 --> 00:10:26,506
British Adviser to the Sultan of Johor
asking about the island's status,

159
00:10:26,586 --> 00:10:29,971
and the Acting State
Secretary of Johor replied

160
00:10:30,051 --> 00:10:34,475
that "the Johore Government
does not claim ownership" of it.

161
00:10:34,475 --> 00:10:38,700
The Court described that
reply as clear in its meaning.

162
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:42,157
It also pointed to acts by
Britain and Singapore that

163
00:10:42,237 --> 00:10:47,161
went beyond running a lighthouse, such
as investigating shipping accidents,

164
00:10:47,241 --> 00:10:49,030
controlling who could visit,

165
00:10:49,110 --> 00:10:53,033
and installing naval communication
equipment, and noted that

166
00:10:53,113 --> 00:10:56,850
Malaysia and its predecessors
had not responded to them.

167
00:10:56,850 --> 00:10:59,931
It concluded that by 1980 sovereignty

168
00:11:00,011 --> 00:11:03,600
over Pedra Branca had passed to Singapore.

169
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:09,028
The vote was 12 to 4. By 15 votes to
1, it awarded Middle Rocks to Malaysia,

170
00:11:09,108 --> 00:11:13,087
and it ruled that South Ledge,
which is above water only

171
00:11:13,167 --> 00:11:17,950
at low tide, belongs to whichever
state owns the waters around it.

172
00:11:17,950 --> 00:11:22,225
The case came back into the news in 2017.

173
00:11:22,225 --> 00:11:26,161
In February that year Malaysia
asked the Court to revise

174
00:11:26,241 --> 00:11:30,106
the 2008 judgment, saying it
had found new documents in

175
00:11:30,186 --> 00:11:33,907
the British National Archives,
and in June it filed a

176
00:11:33,987 --> 00:11:38,075
separate request for the Court
to interpret the judgment.

177
00:11:38,075 --> 00:11:41,847
On the 28th of May 2018
Malaysia told the Court it

178
00:11:41,927 --> 00:11:44,081
would discontinue both cases,

179
00:11:44,161 --> 00:11:49,400
Singapore agreed the next day, and
the Court closed the proceedings.

180
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:53,732
In July 2021 Singapore's
Ministry of National Development

181
00:11:53,812 --> 00:11:57,137
announced development
works at Pedra Branca,

182
00:11:57,217 --> 00:12:02,478
including land reclamation within
half a nautical mile of the island,

183
00:12:02,558 --> 00:12:06,350
to improve maritime safety
and search and rescue.

184
00:12:06,350 --> 00:12:10,389
In Malaysia, a royal
commission of inquiry set up in

185
00:12:10,469 --> 00:12:14,746
2024 examined how the government
had handled the cases,

186
00:12:14,826 --> 00:12:19,025
and its report was made
public in December that year.

187
00:12:19,025 --> 00:12:22,029
The lighthouse that
Thomson built still stands

188
00:12:22,109 --> 00:12:24,925
on the rock and still flashes every night.

189
00:12:24,925 --> 00:12:28,269
Why it matters today:
A lighthouse built in

190
00:12:28,349 --> 00:12:31,852
1851 to stop ships
running aground on a white

191
00:12:31,932 --> 00:12:35,675
rock ended up as part of
the story of a border.

192
00:12:35,675 --> 00:12:40,202
The construction records Thomson
kept, the 1953 letter and a century

193
00:12:40,282 --> 00:12:44,877
of routine acts such as inspecting
the light and investigating wrecks

194
00:12:44,957 --> 00:12:49,213
were all weighed by a court in
The Hague, and the two neighbours

195
00:12:49,293 --> 00:12:53,900
chose to put the question to a court
rather than leave it unsettled.

196
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:57,402
The light also still does
the job it was built for,

197
00:12:57,482 --> 00:13:01,275
at one of the busiest shipping
entrances in the world.
